{"id":"e07t3","name":"Tiered Electricity Bills","lang":"sql","entry":null,"params":[],"limit_s":10,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>An electricity provider bills its customers with a <b>tiered</b> price. You have two tables.</p>\n<p><b>usage</b>: <code>customer_id</code>, <code>month</code> (date, the first day of the month) and <code>kwh</code> (numeric). A customer has at most one row per month.</p>\n<p><b>tiers</b>: <code>tier</code> (integer), <code>from_kwh</code> (numeric), <code>to_kwh</code> (numeric, <code>NULL</code> for the last tier, which has no upper limit) and <code>price</code> (numeric, price of one kWh in this tier). Tiers do not overlap and cover all values from <code>0</code>.</p>\n<p>Each kWh is billed at the price of the tier it falls in: for a usage of <code>u</code> kWh, the quantity billed in a tier is the part of <code>[0, u]</code> that is inside <code>[from_kwh, to_kwh]</code>, which is <code>LEAST(u, to_kwh) - from_kwh</code> when it is positive, and <code>0</code> otherwise. A fixed fee of <code>20</code> is added to every bill, including bills with a usage of <code>0</code>.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return, for every row of <code>usage</code>, the columns <code>customer_id</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>kwh</code> and <code>bill</code> (rounded to 2 decimals), sorted by <code>month</code>, then <code>customer_id</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p>Table <code>usage</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>customer_id</th><th>month</th><th>kwh</th></tr><tr><td>C1</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>250</td></tr><tr><td>C2</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>C3</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C1</td><td>2026-02-01</td><td>200</td></tr><tr><td>C4</td><td>2026-02-01</td><td>100.5</td></tr></table></div><p>Table <code>tiers</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>tier</th><th>from_kwh</th><th>to_kwh</th><th>price</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>0.9</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>100</td><td>200</td><td>1.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>200</td><td>null</td><td>1.5</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>customer_id</th><th>month</th><th>kwh</th><th>bill</th></tr><tr><td>C1</td><td>2026-02-01</td><td>200.0</td><td>220.0</td></tr><tr><td>C4</td><td>2026-02-01</td><td>100.5</td><td>110.55</td></tr><tr><td>C1</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>250.0</td><td>295.0</td></tr><tr><td>C2</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>80.0</td><td>92.0</td></tr><tr><td>C3</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>0.0</td><td>20.0</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>For <code>250</code> kWh with the tiers <code>[0, 100]</code> at <code>0.9</code>, <code>[100, 200]</code> at <code>1.1</code> and <code>[200, ∞)</code> at <code>1.5</code>: <code>100 * 0.9 + 100 * 1.1 + 50 * 1.5 + 20 = 295</code>.</p><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 10 seconds (psql)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 1 GB<br><b>[input]</b> tables <code>usage</code>, <code>tiers</code><br><b>[output]</b> the result of one query with exactly the columns <code>customer_id</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>kwh</code>, <code>bill</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"/* Write your PostgreSQL query here */\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["jointure sur intervalle","LEAST / GREATEST","tranches","LEFT JOIN client sans conso"],"minutes":18,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>query.sql</code>. Efface le commentaire de départ si tu veux.</li><li>Écris <b>une seule requête</b> qui commence par <code>SELECT</code> (ou <code>WITH</code>). Les tables <code>usage</code>, <code>tiers</code> existent déjà, remplies avec les données du test : pas de <code>CREATE TABLE</code> ni d'<code>INSERT</code>.</li><li>Le moteur est PostgreSQL. Si tu écris plusieurs requêtes, seul le résultat de la dernière est noté.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Le résultat de ta requête doit avoir <b>exactement 4 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>customer_id</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>kwh</code>, <code>bill</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Nomme chaque colonne calculée avec <code>AS</code>.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>ORDER BY</code> à la fin de la requête).</li><li>Valeurs : les nombres sont comparés à 0,000001 près : arrondis seulement là où l'énoncé le demande, et au nombre de décimales demandé ; les dates s'affichent AAAA-MM-JJ.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 7 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"tables":{"usage":{"columns":[["customer_id","VARCHAR(5)"],["month","DATE"],["kwh","NUMERIC(10,2)"]],"rows":[["C1","2026-03-01",250],["C2","2026-03-01",80],["C3","2026-03-01",0],["C1","2026-02-01",200],["C4","2026-02-01",100.5]]},"tiers":{"columns":[["tier","INT"],["from_kwh","NUMERIC(10,2)"],["to_kwh","NUMERIC(10,2)"],["price","NUMERIC(6,3)"]],"rows":[[1,0,100,0.9],[2,100,200,1.1],[3,200,null,1.5]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["customer_id","month","kwh","bill"],"rows":[["C1","2026-02-01",200.0,220.0],["C4","2026-02-01",100.5,110.55],["C1","2026-03-01",250.0,295.0],["C2","2026-03-01",80.0,92.0],["C3","2026-03-01",0.0,20.0]]}},{"input":{"tables":{"usage":{"columns":[["customer_id","VARCHAR(5)"],["month","DATE"],["kwh","NUMERIC(10,2)"]],"rows":[["A","2026-01-01",1000]]},"tiers":{"columns":[["tier","INT"],["from_kwh","NUMERIC(10,2)"],["to_kwh","NUMERIC(10,2)"],["price","NUMERIC(6,3)"]],"rows":[[1,0,50,2],[2,50,null,1]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["customer_id","month","kwh","bill"],"rows":[["A","2026-01-01",1000.0,1070.0]]}}],"hidden_n":7,"hidden":"","solution":""}